#!/usr/bin/env bash
# SessionStart hook for neo-dev-toolkit plugin.
# Injects a reminder so Claude proactively invokes the bundled skills
# (neo, brainstorm, improve, openapi-doc, open-collection, confluence-api-doc, gitlab, commit, atlassian)
# whenever a user request matches their triggering contexts.

set -euo pipefail

# Escape a string for safe embedding inside a JSON string literal.
# Uses bash parameter substitution for speed (single C-level pass per rule).
escape_for_json() {
    local s="$1"
    s="${s//\\/\\\\}"
    s="${s//\"/\\\"}"
    s="${s//$'\n'/\\n}"
    s="${s//$'\r'/\\r}"
    s="${s//$'\t'/\\t}"
    printf '%s' "$s"
}

session_context="<EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>
You have the neo-dev-toolkit plugin installed. It bundles nine skills — invoke them via the Skill tool whenever the user's request matches.

1. **\`neo\`** — Route a software-development task to specialist agents (BA, Architect, QA, Developer, Code Reviewer, Security, System Analyzer, Librarian) via phase-based analysis. The Librarian ingests external sources (JIRA / Confluence / image / verbal) once into docs/knowledge/ for every role to reuse. Use for any task that touches AC, system design, code, test cases, API contracts, or security — including single-role calls (สร้าง AC, gen test cases, review PR, fix bug) and multi-role tasks (เพิ่ม endpoint, refactor). Also the single entry point for GitLab MR work — **creating an MR (สร้าง MR) and reviewing an MR (review MR / ตรวจ MR)** in two modes (with a JIRA card → adds AC/TC compliance; without → code + security + regression); it invokes the \`gitlab\` skill for the glab I/O. The skill is an ORCHESTRATOR — do not implement specialist work yourself. Delegate every real task via the \`Agent\` tool (point-to-read), never implement yourself. For **JIRA-card work** it persists a resumable task-file (\`docs/tasks/<card-id>/plan.md\`) + enforces plan+task before coding; resume with \`/neo continue <CARD>\`. Launch with \`/neo\`.

2. **\`brainstorm\`** — ALWAYS use before answering brainstorming, ideation, prompt-crafting, or open-ended exploration (\"brainstorm\", \"ช่วยคิด\", \"I have an idea\", \"improve this prompt\", \"let's explore\", \"I want to build\"). Transforms vague requests into actionable outputs via adaptive guided questioning.

3. **\`improve\`** — Iteratively refine any output until a measurable finish-line condition holds — autonomous improve → self-evaluate loop modeled on Claude Code's \`/goal\`. Establishes ONE condition up front (asks 1-2 questions if vague), then loops without mid-loop checkpoints until met, stalled, or bound reached. Triggers: \"improve this\", \"make it better\", \"iterate\", \"refine\", \"ปรับปรุง\", \"ทำให้ดีขึ้น\".

4. **\`openapi-doc\`** — Generate a single-file **OpenAPI 3.1** spec from Go source at \`bruno/openapi.yaml\` (info/servers/tags + inline paths + \`components.schemas\`), or update/validate against the code. Also emits a dereferenced view at \`bruno/openapi.deref.yaml\` (all internal refs inlined) for viewers that don't expand them. Per-sentinel errors in \`x-error-catalog\`. Built-in three-layer verify (script + fresh-eyes + completeness sweep). Reads Go directly — Go is the source of truth. Triggers: \"gen openapi\", \"สร้าง openapi spec\", \"openapi from go\", \"swagger spec from code\", \"อัปเดต openapi\", \"เช็ค openapi ตรงกับ code\".

5. **\`open-collection\`** — Generate a **runnable** Bruno OpenCollection FROM the \`bruno/openapi.yaml\` spec in two source modes: **Spec** (one request per endpoint, runnable-only — no docs: blocks) or **AC-scenario** (one request per Ready AC carrying \`runtime.assertions\`, joining the spec with neo's \`docs/design/<usecase>/\`). Update/validate too. Three-layer verify. Triggers: \"gen open collection\", \"สร้าง open collection\", \"bruno from openapi\", \"gen scenario collection\", \"สร้าง bruno ตาม AC\", \"AC-based bruno collection\". (Generating the spec → \`openapi-doc\`; the AC/test-case docs → \`neo\`.)

6. **\`confluence-api-doc\`** — Publish API docs to Confluence — from the \`bruno/openapi.yaml\` spec (reads \`x-error-catalog\`) — one endpoint = one page under domain parents, via acli + REST, with pre-flight + round-trip + fresh-eyes + completeness-sweep verify. Triggers: \"publish api doc\", \"sync api doc\", \"push doc to confluence\", \"publish openapi to confluence\", \"อัปเดต api doc ไป confluence\". (Generating the source → \`openapi-doc\`; a runnable collection → \`open-collection\`.)

7. **\`gitlab\`** — Low-level GitLab \`glab\` execution arm — neo invokes it for MR create + review-comment posting; usable directly for lightweight ops: read/summarize an MR, update MR description, list MRs, view MR/CI status, fetch CI logs, approve MRs. Triggers: a bare GitLab MR URL, \"อ่าน MR\", \"ดู MR\", \"อัพเดท MR\", \"list MRs\", \"check pipeline\", \"approve MR\". NOTE: creating, reviewing, fixing, or addressing feedback on an MR route through \`neo\` — not here.

8. **\`commit\`** — Smart git commit workflow with protected-branch guard, rebase onto base, secret-aware staging, and conventional commit messages. ALWAYS use this skill whenever the user asks to commit — do not run \`git commit\` directly. Triggers: \"commit\", \"/commit\", \"commit changes\", \"save my work\", \"push this\", \"commit and push\", \"commit ให้หน่อย\", \"ช่วย commit\", \"เสร็จแล้ว\", \"save and push\".

9. **\`atlassian\`** — Drive Jira + Confluence from the terminal via the \`acli\` CLI: view/search/create/edit/transition/assign Jira work items, manage sprints/boards/projects, read Confluence pages + manage spaces. A thin shell over \`acli --help\` — it carries the command map plus the JQL/workflow/safety judgment \`--help\` can't, and is the acli reference \`neo\` points to. Triggers: \"ดู issue ของฉัน\", \"view my issues\", \"transition ไป In Progress\", \"สร้าง bug ใน Jira\", \"search ด้วย JQL\", \"ดู sprint ปัจจุบัน\", \"ดู Confluence page\", \"list spaces\", or any raw acli op. NOTE: verifying AC/TC/design against a JIRA card → \`neo\`; publishing API docs to Confluence → \`confluence-api-doc\`.

Single-file fixes, quick questions, and pure research tasks do NOT need these skills — answer directly.
</EXTREMELY_IMPORTANT>"

context_escaped=$(escape_for_json "$session_context")

# Emit the Claude Code hook output shape.
printf '{\n  "hookSpecificOutput": {\n    "hookEventName": "SessionStart",\n    "additionalContext": "%s"\n  }\n}\n' "$context_escaped"

exit 0
